    "question": {
      ":question/id": "ed873b4e-f115-56f5-bc6b-fe0e0e82da1d",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=9 + \\frac{4 y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $y=3 x^{4} - 2 x + 1$\n- **II.** $y=- x^{4} - 3 x$\n- **III.** $y=- x^{4} - 3 x + 1$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the stated domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=3 x^{4} - 2 x + 1,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=12 x^{3} - 2.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =9 + \\frac{4 y}{x} \\\\ 12 x^{3} - 2 & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4\\cdot \\left(3 x^{4} - 2 x + 1\\right)}{x}+9 \\\\ 12 x^{3} - 2 & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{12 x^{4} + x + 4}{x} \\\\ -3 - \\frac{4}{x} & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=3 x^{4} - 2 x + 1$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=- x^{4} - 3 x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- 4 x^{3} - 3.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =9 + \\frac{4 y}{x} \\\\ - 4 x^{3} - 3 & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4\\cdot \\left(- x^{4} - 3 x\\right)}{x}+9 \\\\ - 4 x^{3} - 3 & =- 4 x^{3} - 3.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=- x^{4} - 3 x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=- x^{4} - 3 x + 1,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- 4 x^{3} - 3.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =9 + \\frac{4 y}{x} \\\\ - 4 x^{3} - 3 & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4\\cdot \\left(- x^{4} - 3 x + 1\\right)}{x}+9 \\\\ - 4 x^{3} - 3 & \\overset{?}{=}- 4 x^{3} - 3 + \\frac{4}{x} \\\\ - \\frac{4}{x} & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=- x^{4} - 3 x + 1$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
