  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186100959,
      "id": "80ed79a9-8e39-5c84-9c87-dec266060afc",
      "title": "Identifying Solutions to First-Order Differential Equations"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "04db2e23-594d-5bf8-b806-482e24f7d03f",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3 + \\frac{4 y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $y=- x^{4} - x - 1$\n- **II.** $y=- 3 x^{4} - x + 1$\n- **III.** $y=- x^{4} - x$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the stated domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=- x^{4} - x - 1,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- 4 x^{3} - 1.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3 + \\frac{4 y}{x} \\\\ - 4 x^{3} - 1 & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4\\cdot \\left(- x^{4} - x - 1\\right)}{x}+3 \\\\ - 4 x^{3} - 1 & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{- 4 x^{4} - x - 4}{x} \\\\ \\frac{4}{x} & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=- x^{4} - x - 1$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=- 3 x^{4} - x + 1,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- 12 x^{3} - 1.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3 + \\frac{4 y}{x} \\\\ - 12 x^{3} - 1 & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4\\cdot \\left(- 3 x^{4} - x + 1\\right)}{x}+3 \\\\ - 12 x^{3} - 1 & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{- 12 x^{4} - x + 4}{x} \\\\ - \\frac{4}{x} & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=- 3 x^{4} - x + 1$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=- x^{4} - x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- 4 x^{3} - 1.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3 + \\frac{4 y}{x} \\\\ - 4 x^{3} - 1 & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4\\cdot \\left(- x^{4} - x\\right)}{x}+3 \\\\ - 4 x^{3} - 1 & =- 4 x^{3} - 1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=- x^{4} - x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "68b5ff92-da84-55a9-89cf-d71bb77ebc67",
