  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186100959,
      "id": "80ed79a9-8e39-5c84-9c87-dec266060afc",
      "title": "Identifying Solutions to First-Order Differential Equations"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "4c02336f-cb71-5e8a-b513-2c13dc72dfea",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-3 + \\frac{2 y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $y=- x^{2} + 3 x$\n- **II.** $y=x^{2} + 3 x + 1$\n- **III.** $y=- 3 x^{2} + 4 x + 1$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the stated domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=- x^{2} + 3 x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3 - 2 x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-3 + \\frac{2 y}{x} \\\\ 3 - 2 x & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2\\cdot \\left(- x^{2} + 3 x\\right)}{x}-3 \\\\ 3 - 2 x & =3 - 2 x.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=- x^{2} + 3 x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=x^{2} + 3 x + 1,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2 x + 3.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-3 + \\frac{2 y}{x} \\\\ 2 x + 3 & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2\\cdot \\left(x^{2} + 3 x + 1\\right)}{x}-3 \\\\ 2 x + 3 & \\overset{?}{=}2 x + 3 + \\frac{2}{x} \\\\ - \\frac{2}{x} & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=x^{2} + 3 x + 1$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=- 3 x^{2} + 4 x + 1,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4 - 6 x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-3 + \\frac{2 y}{x} \\\\ 4 - 6 x & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2\\cdot \\left(- 3 x^{2} + 4 x + 1\\right)}{x}-3 \\\\ 4 - 6 x & \\overset{?}{=}- 6 x + 5 + \\frac{2}{x} \\\\ \\frac{- x - 2}{x} & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=- 3 x^{2} + 4 x + 1$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "bdea41c2-ceb0-5bdf-97e7-64a9418a7ded",
