  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186100959,
      "id": "80ed79a9-8e39-5c84-9c87-dec266060afc",
      "title": "Identifying Solutions to First-Order Differential Equations"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "dc79644b-71eb-5456-9a3a-8e2a090cebb6",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3 + \\frac{2 y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $y=- x^{2} - 3 x - 1$\n- **II.** $y=2 x^{2} - 2 x$\n- **III.** $y=- 2 x^{2} - 3 x$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the stated domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=- x^{2} - 3 x - 1,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- 2 x - 3.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3 + \\frac{2 y}{x} \\\\ - 2 x - 3 & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2\\cdot \\left(- x^{2} - 3 x - 1\\right)}{x}+3 \\\\ - 2 x - 3 & \\overset{?}{=}- 2 x - 3 - \\frac{2}{x} \\\\ \\frac{2}{x} & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=- x^{2} - 3 x - 1$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=2 x^{2} - 2 x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4 x - 2.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3 + \\frac{2 y}{x} \\\\ 4 x - 2 & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2\\cdot \\left(2 x^{2} - 2 x\\right)}{x}+3 \\\\ 4 x - 2 & \\overset{?}{=}4 x - 1 \\\\ -1 & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=2 x^{2} - 2 x$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=- 2 x^{2} - 3 x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- 4 x - 3.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3 + \\frac{2 y}{x} \\\\ - 4 x - 3 & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2\\cdot \\left(- 2 x^{2} - 3 x\\right)}{x}+3 \\\\ - 4 x - 3 & =- 4 x - 3.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=- 2 x^{2} - 3 x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
