  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186100959,
      "id": "80ed79a9-8e39-5c84-9c87-dec266060afc",
      "title": "Identifying Solutions to First-Order Differential Equations"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "6a24b740-77be-5b21-9a21-98f213861ab2",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-1 + \\frac{2 y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $y=x^{2} + x$\n- **II.** $y=- 3 x^{2} + 2 x - 1$\n- **III.** $y=3 x^{2} + x$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the stated domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=x^{2} + x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2 x + 1.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-1 + \\frac{2 y}{x} \\\\ 2 x + 1 & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2\\cdot \\left(x^{2} + x\\right)}{x}-1 \\\\ 2 x + 1 & =2 x + 1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=x^{2} + x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=- 3 x^{2} + 2 x - 1,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2 - 6 x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-1 + \\frac{2 y}{x} \\\\ 2 - 6 x & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2\\cdot \\left(- 3 x^{2} + 2 x - 1\\right)}{x}-1 \\\\ 2 - 6 x & \\overset{?}{=}- 6 x + 3 - \\frac{2}{x} \\\\ \\frac{2 - x}{x} & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=- 3 x^{2} + 2 x - 1$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=3 x^{2} + x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=6 x + 1.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-1 + \\frac{2 y}{x} \\\\ 6 x + 1 & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2\\cdot \\left(3 x^{2} + x\\right)}{x}-1 \\\\ 6 x + 1 & =6 x + 1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=3 x^{2} + x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "3761dcea-8f78-5a01-8821-223e2b6f166a",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/checkbox",
